WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.970
It's been nearly 50 years since Bruce Lee
died suddenly in the prime of his life, but

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:08.020
his legend lives on in ways that even he could
never have imagined.

00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:12.380
Here's a look at the highlights and lowlights
of his long and varied career, from the movies

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:15.680
that made him an icon to the ones we'd rather
just forget.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.579
Game of Death was a movie famously built around
the footage Bruce Lee left behind from his

00:00:20.579 --> 00:00:24.739
unfinished final film, most of which consisted
of fight scenes that took place on various

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:25.900
levels of a pagoda.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.739
After Lee's death, Enter the Dragon director
Robert Clouse took that footage and integrated

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:35.260
it into a new story about a martial arts movie
star being hunted by a crime syndicate.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.039
Game of Death is also notorious for integrating
footage of Lee from other movies, often to

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:45.040
jarring effect, and at one point even uses
footage of Lee's actual body in his casket.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.900
If Game of Death had simply been made as a
'70s martial arts flick full of subterfuge

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.480
and slick fight scenes, it wouldn't be a bad
movie at all.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:56.220
As it is, the footage that doesn't try to
trick you into thinking you're watching Bruce

00:00:56.239 --> 00:01:00.589
Lee is solid, the opening credits are killer,
and John Barry's score is probably the best

00:01:00.589 --> 00:01:01.140
thing about it.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.960
Watching it in context as a film cobbled together
from Lee's unfinished work, however, makes

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.660
it a trying viewing experience, and a sad
reminder of what might have been.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.540
The Game of Death was supposed to star Bruce
Lee as a retired martial artist who is coerced

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:18.700
into retrieving something valuable from the
top of a mysterious pagoda — with each level

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.360
guarded by a martial artist with a different
fighting style.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:25.360
As you know, however, the true version of
the movie never came to fruition, thanks to

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.840
Lee's untimely passing.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.840
What footage did exist was later released
as part of the documentary Bruce Lee: A Warrior's

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:32.360
Journey.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:36.960
The Game of Death's climax featured Lee in
his now-iconic yellow tracksuit, taking on

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:41.710
these martial artists in a series of precisely
choreographed battles, culminating in a fight

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:44.500
with Kareem Abdul-Jabbar's character at the
pagoda's summit.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.920
Watching what exists of The Game of Death
is a bittersweet experience, because while

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:52.570
we'll never get to see the full film, seeing
what Lee was able to do with this level of

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:56.180
creative control and precision is truly astonishing
nonetheless.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:01.590
Bruce Lee's first major introduction to American
audiences was as Kato in The Green Hornet,

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:05.680
the superhero action-adventure series in which
he stole the show as sidekick to the titular

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:06.420
masked hero.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:11.880
The show was successful in America, but overseas
it made Lee an even bigger star, to the point

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.720
that in Hong Kong it became known as "The
Kato Show."

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:19.670
Lee's international popularity as Kato, combined
with the mythology that sprung up around him

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:25.200
after his untimely death in 1973, led Fox
to consider repurposing the old Green Hornet

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.140
episodes to capitalize on his fame.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:33.700
So, in 1974, they cut together several episodes
from the 1966-67 series and released them

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:34.800
as a feature film.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.360
It did well in some markets, but as you might
expect from a heavily episodic series like

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.460
The Green Hornet, it doesn't actually work
all that well as a movie with a narrative

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:44.220
throughline.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.260
The Way of the Dragon is the first film in
which Bruce Lee officially stepped into the

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:53.490
role of both director and screenwriter, giving
him more creative control than he had on any

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:54.720
of his previous works.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:59.180
The story is relatively simple: a martial
artist heads to a foreign land to become an

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:03.090
enforcer in a fight to save innocent people
from a tyrannical crime boss.

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:08.240
However, more than any of his previous films,
this one is truly infused with Lee's personality,

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.040
sense of humor, and sense of pacing.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:14.520
Because of that, Way of the Dragon is funnier
than Lee's previous Hong Kong films, as he

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.820
plays the fish out of water with a huge amount
of fun.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:21.880
It's also got a more ambitious sense of action,
as exemplified by the scenes in which he single-handedly

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:25.420
takes on a gang of thugs inside the restaurant
at the center of the conflict.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:29.700
Most famously, though, this is the film in
which Bruce Lee fights Chuck Norris, who made

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.000
his big-screen debut with Way of the Dragon.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.400
That fight, which is set inside Rome's Colosseum,
still holds up as one of the coolest things

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.460
to have ever happened in martial arts cinema.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:51.600
Bruce Lee's first major leading role in a
martial arts film was in The Big Boss, the

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:55.180
film he made after returning to Hong Kong
following a short and frustrating attempt

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:56.080
to break Hollywood.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:00.660
Lee plays Cheng Chao-an, a young man who moves
from China to Thailand to live with family

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:05.020
members who are unknowingly connected to a
drug smuggling ring run out of an ice factory.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:09.340
Things start to get rough almost immediately,
but Cheng can't join the fight because of

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:12.580
a promise he made to his mother to never get
involved in violence.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:15.780
Let's be clear: The Big Boss is actually a
pretty good movie.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:19.800
It just happens to be the worst of the major
martial arts films that Lee had completed

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:20.780
at the time of his death.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:25.830
There are some terrific fight scenes here,
and Lee's onscreen swagger is palpable.

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:29.349
When viewed alongside his later work, though,
you can easily see just how much further he

00:04:29.349 --> 00:04:30.400
could really push himself.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:35.520
Sadly, it's impossible to separate the release of Enter the Dragon from the death of its star.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.020
Bruce Lee died just weeks before this film
was released in America, and while it was

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:43.889
supposed to make him a global star by bringing
his Hong Kong kung fu stylings to a bigger

00:04:43.889 --> 00:04:46.600
audience, in death it made him an icon.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:51.900
Enter the Dragon remains one of the most culturally
significant films of the 1970s, a watershed

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:56.099
moment that informed and influenced countless
martial arts films that came after, as well

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.640
as action cinema in general.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:02.279
Even when you try to see the film outside
the context of Lee's death, you'll still experience

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:03.300
a masterpiece.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:07.280
Enter the Dragon is the work of a young star
who poured everything he'd learned from his

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:10.460
past hits into this one, perfection-seeking
megahit.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:15.180
It remains a riveting film, from Lee's "emotional
content" speech near the beginning all the

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:18.440
way to that legendary mirrored room fight
scene at the end.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:20.300
"What was that?

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:21.960
An exhibition?

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:25.800
We need emotional content."

